    "result_text": "`python -m app.server --http` now serves the inventory server over MCP Streamable HTTP. I tested it end to end with a real MCP client and it works.\n\n**The change** (`app/server.py:28`):\n```python\ndef serve_http(host: str, port: int) -> None:\n    build_server().run(\"streamable-http\", host=host, port=port)\n```\nI checked the installed `mcp==2.2.0` source to confirm how this works. The server listens at the default `/mcp` path and runs uvicorn, which keeps the process running until it's stopped, just like the stdio mode. I didn't touch the stdio path or add any dependencies.\n\n**Testing:**\n- `pytest -q`: 2 passed.\n- I started `python -m app.server --http --port 8765` and connected over HTTP to `http://127.0.0.1:8765/mcp` with the SDK's Streamable HTTP client. Setup worked, the tool list showed `['stock', 'reserve']`, `stock(\"MS-330\")` returned `42`, and `reserve(\"MS-330\", 2)` returned `40`.\n\n**Needs your action:** my permission settings blocked the cleanup commands, so two things are left over:\n- The test server is still running in the background on port 8765. Stop it, or end its task.\n- The throwaway test script `_smoke_client.py` is still in the project root. Please delete it.\n\n**Before remote agents can connect:** the default host is `127.0.0.1`, which only accepts local connections. Remote agents need something like `--host 0.0.0.0`. On localhost, the SDK automatically restricts which Host and Origin headers it accepts. On any other host that restriction is off and there's no authentication. If this will be reachable beyond a trusted network, it should go behind a proxy that handles auth and TLS."
